/* glossary */
var GL=`Arawakan|The language family of Garifuna (North Arawak / Ta-Maipurean branch). Garifuna is the only Arawakan language still spoken in Central America.
Balliceaux|The small island off Saint Vincent where Garifuna were held before the 1797 deportation to Roatán.
Black Carib|A British colonial term for community members of more pronounced African appearance. Most community members consider it pejorative, though it persists in historical literature.
Buyei|One spiritual, medical and historical specialist. The plural is Buyein.
Buyein|Plural of Buyei. Spiritual, medical and historical specialists, the closest Garifuna institutional equivalent to natural philosophers.
Caribania|The confederacy of chiefdoms and captaincies on the Central American mainland that succeeded Youloumain.
Chatoyer|Paramount chief of the Garifuna on Saint Vincent during the Carib Wars.
Dori|A dugout canoe, the foundational Garifuna vessel inherited from Kalinago and Arawakan boat-building tradition.
Dügü|The central Garifuna mortuary ceremony, which summons and honours ancestral spirits over several days.
Garifuna / Garinagu|Garifuna names one person or the language; Garinagu is the plural, naming the people collectively.
Gubida|Ancestral spirits summoned and propitiated during the Dügü ceremony.
Hairoun / Yurumein|The Kalinago and Garifuna name for the island of Saint Vincent.
Igneri / Iñeri|The name for Island Carib speakers. Island Carib is the extinct language closest to Garifuna.
Kalinago|The Island Caribs of Saint Vincent and the Lesser Antilles, the foundational nucleus of the Garifuna.
Karifuna|The indigenous self-designation from which the name Garifuna derives.
Morenos|The Spanish colonial classification for the Garifuna, legally distinct from Negros, which denoted enslaved status.
Pipante|A larger, ocean-going canoe able to carry cargo and crews of six or more.
Poito|The Garifuna kinship-assimilation system through which outsiders could be formally incorporated into the clan.
Alejo Beni|Nineteenth-century Garifuna leader who led the 1823 arrival at Stann Creek (Dangriga), Belize, and is remembered in Settlement Day traditions.
Settlement Day|The Belizean commemoration of Garifuna arrival, linked in local histories to the Alejo Beni tradition.
Walumugu|The name by which the Garifuna general Juan Francisco Bulnes is remembered from the independence era.
Yellow Caribs|The colonial label for community members of more pronounced Kalinago appearance.
Youloumain|The paramount Garifuna-Kalinago chiefdom on Saint Vincent, led by Chatoyer.`.split('\n').map(function(l){var i=l.indexOf('|');return[l.slice(0,i),l.slice(i+1)]});
function gdraw(){var q=$('#gq').value.toLowerCase(),r=GL.filter(function(g){return(g[0]+' '+g[1]).toLowerCase().indexOf(q)>-1});$('#gcount').textContent=r.length+' of '+GL.length+' terms';$('#glist').innerHTML=r.length?r.map(function(g){return'<dt>'+esc(g[0])+'</dt><dd>'+esc(g[1])+'</dd>'}).join(''):'<dd>No terms match. Clear the search to see all terms.</dd>'}
